findings.failure_scenario:
@sync
class Token:
    value: int = 0
    fn init(mut self):
        while register(self):
            pass
        self.value = 2

fn register(t: Token) -> bool:
    # stores t where another thread can reach it
    return false
This is accepted: the whole-self use in the while condition is forgotten, so `self.value = 2` after publication gets no E7003. The same happens with `for i in 0..2: self.value = i; register(self)`. Once threads exist, another thread can read `value` while init writes it, which is a data race in Safe code (THR-13).

@sync
class Cfg:
    a: int
    b: int
    fn init(mut self, n: int):
        self.a = match n:
            0 => 1
            _ => 2
        self.b = 3
Both assignments get `E7003 cannot write field ... after using self as a whole`, although self is never used as a whole. The THR-1 rule is violated in the other direction: a valid program is rejected.

@sync
class Conn:
    fd: int
    fn drop(mut self):
        close(self.fd)
This is E7003, so a @sync class cannot release a resource in drop. Whether that is intended belongs in the owner queue, not in an implementation choice.

class Holder:
    buf: [u8; 2]

fn text(b: Span[u8]) -> str:
    return b.to_str().unwrap()

fn main():
    h = Holder([0xC3, 0xA9])   # "é", valid
    alias = h
    t = text(h.buf)            # str over h.buf; Copy field, no access word
    alias.buf = [0x41, 0xF0]   # instantaneous Copy write, never checked
    for c in t.chars():        # decodes 0xF0 as a 4-byte lead and reads 3 bytes past the 2-byte view
        println(c)

The static borrow checker does not see h and alias as one object. At run time nothing checks, so the program reads out of bounds, which is undefined behaviour in Safe code. The spec's [EXC-17] and [TXT-1]/[TXT-5] genuinely conflict here, so an ODR is needed (for example: to_str on a view that may observe unchecked writes copies or is rejected, or a view of a Copy class field is not a valid to_str source). The TXT-2 tests only use local Arrays.

class Bag:
    items: Array[int]
    fn init(mut self):
        self.items = []
    fn reset(self):
        self.items = []          # allowed by D-362
    fn size(self) -> int:
        return self.items.len()
    fn refill(mut self):
        if self.size() > 0:      # read access on items vs the caller's WRITER -> panic
            self.reset()

fn main():
    b = Bag()
    b.refill()

This should run. By this reading of the code it panics 'exclusivity violation ... Bag.items'. Whether [EXC-5] ('accesses nested inside the same mut self method are reborrows') covers a plain call on self is ambiguous: record it as an ODR or fix it in the compiler. Either way it needs a test.

#! module overflow(wrap)
const D: i8 = i8.MIN // -1i8      # folds to -128
fn q(a: i8, b: i8) -> i8:
    return a // b
fn main():
    println(D, q(i8.MIN, -1i8))
This prints -128 for D and then panics with `integer overflow in //` for q, instead of printing -128 -128. The same happens for `//=` and for i128.

@overflow(wrap)
fn total() -> i8:
    return sum([100i8, 100i8])
fn main():
    println(total())
This prints -56 (under saturate: 127) instead of panicking with an integer overflow. Nothing signals the wrong result.

@overflow(wrap)
fn f() -> void:
    println(127i8.add(1i8))   # prints -128 (caller policy)
    println(2i8.pow(7))       # panics (fixed contract)
Two integer methods that TYP-21 calls 'the operator' behave oppositely under the same caller policy. Whether `x.add(y)` should panic here is a question the ODR was meant to settle and did not.

class Bag: items: Array[int]; fn init(mut self): self.items = [1, 2, 3]
fn show(v: Span[int], other: Bag):
    other.items = []     # word is 0: main's read already ended
    println(v[0])        # reads the freed buffer
fn main(): b = Bag(); c = b; v = b.items[..]; show(v, c)
The same happens with `grow(b.items, c)` where `fn grow(mut xs: Array[int], other: Bag): for x in other.items: xs.push(x)`, and with `show(b.view(), c)` for a getter (the skip path). No existing test passes a field view to a user function as its last use.

class Person: first: String; last: String; ...
    fn names(self) -> (str, str): return (self.first, self.last)
    fn rename(mut self): self.first = "x"
fn main(): p = Person(); pair = p.names(); q = p; q.rename(); println(pair[0])
`q.rename()`'s object write finds every word 0, frees the old `first`, and `pair[0]` reads freed memory. This also applies to a struct result with two view fields.

class Person: name: String; fn get_name(self) -> str: return self.name
    fn greet(mut self): println(self.get_name())
fn main(): p = Person(); p.greet()
This panics "read access to Person.name while a write access ... is active". Likewise `fn items_mut(mut self) -> MutSpan[int]` called as `v = c.items_mut()` panics before returning. None of the three EXC-18 tests calls a getter inside a `mut self` method.

class Bag: items: Array[int] ...
    fn total(mut self) -> int:
        count = fn() => len(self.items)
        return count()
This panics "read access to Bag.items while a write access ... is active", assuming the closure may capture `self`. Untested.
